# README

## Supports

NixOS, and Stand-alone for a Linux

## Standalone Home Manager

setup

```bash
git clone https://github.com/rei78-4e/dotfiles
cd ~/dotfiles

build

```bash
home-manager switch --flake ".#$USER"
```

## NixOS

there are `hosts/desktop` and `hosts/thinkpad/`.

Machine-specific settings live in the host directory; shared desktop setup lives in `modules/desktop.nix`.

### Initial Setup (per host)

Clone this repo to `~/dotfiles`, then generate and copy the hardware configuration:

```bash
sudo nixos-generate-config --dir /etc/nixos
sudo cp /etc/nixos/hardware-configuration.nix ~/dotfiles/hosts/thinkpad/
sudo cp /etc/nixos/hardware-configuration.nix ~/dotfiles/hosts/desktop/
```

```bash
cd ~/dotfiles
sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#thinkpad   # on the ThinkPad
sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#desktop    # on the desktop
```


## Rebuild (Apply Changes)

**Standalone Linux (Debian, Ubuntu, etc.)**

```bash
cd ~/dotfiles
home-manager switch --flake ".#$USER"
```

**NixOS**

```bash
cd ~/dotfiles
sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#thinkpad   # on the ThinkPad
sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#desktop    # on the desktop
```

there are `rebuild` function in fish.

## Update Packages (Flake Inputs)

To update the underlying packages (nixpkgs, etc.) to their latest versions:

```bash
cd ~/dotfiles
nix flake update
```

there are `update` function in fish.

## Dependency Graphs

`script/nix-deps-graph.py` renders dependency graphs as SVG (or PNG/DOT).
Arrows point at what a component depends on.

```bash
# one overview: flake inputs -> NixOS output -> runtime closure
./script/nix-deps-graph.py combined

# all three graphs (flake / system / overview) -> graph/*.svg
./script/nix-deps-graph.py all

# flake input lock graph only
./script/nix-deps-graph.py flake

# runtime closure of /run/current-system, direct dependencies only
./script/nix-deps-graph.py system --depth 1

# start from a package in the closure and go two levels deep
./script/nix-deps-graph.py system --root helix --depth 2

# focus on a few packages
./script/nix-deps-graph.py system --depth 3 --include 'glibc|systemd|linux'

# other output options
./script/nix-deps-graph.py system --format png -o system.png
./script/nix-deps-graph.py system --rankdir TB --exclude 'tree-sitter'
```

Useful options:

- `--depth N` / `-d N` – levels below the root (default `1`, `-1` = unlimited)
- `--root PATH|NAME` – any store path or package name in the closure
- `--include REGEX`, `--exclude REGEX` – filter package labels
- `--max-nodes N` – safety limit (default `1000`)
- `--format svg|png|dot`, `--rankdir TB|LR|BT|RL`, `--no-dot`
- `--flake DIR|URL`, `-o/--output FILE|DIR`

The `combined` graph places the flake lock graph next to the running
system's store path and its runtime closure, so the flake inputs, the
pinned `nixpkgs`, and the resulting NixOS system are visible in one SVG.

Graphviz is used for layout. It is looked up on `PATH` and in the Nix store;
if it is missing, the script builds `nixpkgs#graphviz` once with `nix build`.

## Config Canvas

`script/config-canvas/server.py` is a local, dependency-free atlas of this
repository. It rescans the tree on every run, opens itself with `xdg-open`,
and exits when the browser tab goes away.

```bash
./script/config-canvas/server.py

# options
./script/config-canvas/server.py --no-open --port 8765
./script/config-canvas/server.py ~/dotfiles
./script/config-canvas/server.py --check
```

The canvas follows the Paper Design palette (paper / ink / rose, flat
surfaces, hairline rules). Files view groups every node by folder colour,
includes root-level files directly under the root folder node, and draws
curved containment lines.
Hover any node for a dossier (size, lines, words, mode, owner, mtime,
preview). Drag a node to pull its neighbours through the spring lines;
right-drag anywhere to pan. `ARRANGE` (or `A`) randomises the constellation.
`CONTENT` (`2`) parses the selected file into a second node graph: nix
attrsets/bindings/lambdas/imports, JSON/TOML/Markdown, or a text outline.
Textual files get source fragments; images get thumbnails; audio/video and
PDFs get previews or direct links; binaries get a hex view.

`FLOW` (`3`) is the rebuild pipeline: `flake.lock` inputs -> flake outputs ->
host/home entrypoints -> imported modules -> per-output evaluation ->
`nixos-rebuild` / `home-manager switch` -> system generation. Import edges
are pulled from every reachable `.nix` file, and each output shows the
modules in its evaluation closure.


